      "problem": "Find the minimum value of $y$ on the graph $y=|x+4|-1.$",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that $y=|x-h|+k$ is a vertical translation of the absolute value graph $y=|x|$ by $h$ units right and $k$ units up.\n\nThe function $y=|x+4|-1$ has $h=-4$ and $k=-1.$ Therefore, it is a translation of the absolute value graph $y=|x|$ by $4$ units *left* and $1$ unit *down*.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14250789/assets/45411bac1f347093b8b9931e1f8f8bee4f1eb411b6256a4c56d0ba33cffe603e.png)\n\nLooking at the graph, we can see that the minimum value of $y$ is $-1.$ This occurs at the point $(-4,-1).$",
